why 1 1/4 RANGE The bass drops an octave to a deep D under the held tonic chord: a dark opening.
why 1 3/8 DOUBLING A passing chord over a held bass; its doubling follows the upper voices' steps.
why 2 0 HARMONIC_RHYTHM D minor carries over the bar line while soprano and alto keep climbing. The opening dwells on the tonic, decorated by passing notes.
why 4 1/2 RANGE At the fermata the bass drops to a deep D for a full cadence.
why 4 1/2 SPACING The deep bass D leaves a wide gap under the tenor at the fermata: a full, dark cadence chord.
why 4 1/2 LEAPS The bass falls A–D and then drops an octave: the cadence's root, first in the normal register, then deeper.
why 9 1/4 RANGE The bass leaps up an octave on the D minor chord and walks down D–C–B♭: a short high stretch.
why 14 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION All voices rise into the next chord: the alto's F♯ resolves up to G, and the others step or leap up with the climbing tune.
why 15 1/2 TENDENCY The tenor's E♭ skips down to C and A instead of falling by step, outlining the diminished chord before rising to D.
why 15 1/2 LEAPS The tenor falls E♭–C–A through the notes of a diminished chord. Its ends form a tritone, but each move is a third through chord notes.
why 16 0 SEVENTH The chord before is the dominant of G minor, D–F♯–A, with the soprano's B♭ leaning on it. The tenor's A is its fifth, not a seventh, so it is free to move.
why 16 0 TENDENCY The alto's F♯, leading tone of G, drops to D, the fifth of the G minor chord at the fermata. The soprano takes G, so the alto completes the chord.
why 16 0 SIMILAR_MOTION A cadence on G minor where every voice falls: soprano B♭–G, alto F♯–D, tenor D–B♭ and the bass to a low G.
why 16 0 DIRECT The tune falls B♭–G while the bass leaps D–G into the fermata: hidden octaves at a cadence, where the given melody leaves little choice.
why 18 1/2 DOUBLING The tune falls C–A while the alto steps G–A, so both share A, the third of the F chord.
why 23 1/4 SIX_FOUR A passing ⁶₄ on the weak second beat, as the bass moves G–C–B♭–A toward the cadence.
why 30 0 UNRESOLVED_NCT The tenor holds E over the bar line into the D minor chord and falls to D: a suspension, prepared, tied and resolved by step.
why 31 0 HARMONIC_RHYTHM The B♭ chord arrives on the last beat and carries over the bar line while the bass walks down A–G–F. The decoration, not a new chord, carries the motion.
why 31 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano and alto share E, the root of the diminished chord, on its way to the dominant. The soprano's E is the tune and the alto steps F–E beneath it; in exercises, double the third instead.
